+# Research: category deletion
+
+## Request path and current behavior
+
+- `sm-shop/.../CategoryApi.java` maps `DELETE /api/v1/private/category/{id}` to `CategoryFacade.deleteCategory(id, merchantStore)` and returns 200 on success. The facade validates the category through `CategoryFacadeImpl.getOne(...)`, so a missing root category is intended to become `ResourceNotFoundException`/404.
+- `GET /api/v1/category/{id}` calls `CategoryFacadeImpl.getById(...)`; it loads the category by language, then loads descendants by lineage. The same facade also builds the category hierarchy from `CategoryService` store queries.
+- `GET /api/v1/products?category={id}` places the id in `ProductCriteria.categoryIds`; the product repository filters on the `PRODUCT_CATEGORY` association. Therefore removing associations makes the deleted category's product listing empty without deleting the product.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`sm-core/.../CategoryServiceImpl.java`, method `delete(Category)`, collects the root and descendants, queries products in those category ids, and then:
+
+1. If a product has more than one category it removes the target categories and updates it.
+2. Otherwise it calls `productService.delete(dbProduct)`, which removes the product and all product-owned data, including stock/availability and other product state. This is the direct cause of products disappearing and of carts/orders losing the product they reference.
+3. The condition checking whether categories remain uses the earlier `product` instance rather than the refreshed `dbProduct`, so it cannot reliably prevent deletion.
+
+The `Product` model stores categories as a lazy `@ManyToMany` through `PRODUCT_CATEGORY`; category deletion must only remove rows in that relationship. `ShoppingCartItem` stores a product id and SKU as scalar columns rather than an entity foreign key, and `OrderProduct` stores the SKU/name/price snapshot. Neither should be changed by category deletion; deleting the product is what breaks those workflows.
+
+## Category hierarchy and deletion effects
+
+`Category.categories` is a `@OneToMany(mappedBy="parent", cascade=CascadeType.REMOVE)`, so deleting the root can cascade to descendants. The current service nevertheless gathers descendants for product cleanup and only explicitly deletes the root. The safe operation is to remove every affected category from every affected product, flush/update those products, then delete the category subtree; no product delete is needed.
+
+`CategoryRepository.findByLineage(...)` returns descendants using the category lineage, and `CategoryRepository.findById(Integer storeId, Long categoryId, ...)`/`findByIdAndStore(...)` scope lookups to the merchant. The facade's `getOne(...)` currently calls `categoryService.getById(categoryId)` without the store id, so the deletion lookup should use the store-scoped lookup already used by `getById(store,id)`. This also ensures a category missing from the current store is a 404 rather than an accidental cross-store operation.
+
+## Files expected to change
+
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java`: preserve products while removing target category links, and delete the complete category subtree.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/facade/category/CategoryFacadeImpl.java`: use the store-scoped existence check for deletion so nonexistent categories return 404 consistently.
+- Category integration tests under `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/integration/category/` (or a focused service test if the existing integration fixture is insufficient): cover product preservation, subtree cleanup, 404 category reads/listings, and nonexistent deletion.
+
+## Verification considerations
+
+The repository uses Maven multi-module builds. Relevant checks should include compilation and the category integration tests. A test should assert that a product assigned only to the deleted subtree still has its SKU/product record and no deleted categories, while a product assigned to another category retains that category; it should also exercise cart/order behavior if the existing test support makes those fixtures available.
